  "live_records": {
    "q-106067": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106067",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-2⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-2)-f(-1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,-2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩) \\\\ ((-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+1)-({1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 9-3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,2y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =⟨2{c}_{1},2{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-3⟩ \\\\ 6 & =6{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2} \\\\ 1 & ={c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{-2-1} & =\\frac{x-(-1)}{2-(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{(-3)} & =\\frac{x+1}{3} \\\\ y-1 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =-{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation ${c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-(-{c}_{1}) & =1 \\\\ 2{c}_{1} & =1 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
