    "q-106091": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106091",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Determine the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=y-{x}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-2,-8⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=y-{x}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-2,-8⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0)-f(-2,-8) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0⟩-⟨-2,-8⟩) \\\\ (0-{1}^{3})-(-8-(-2)^{3}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-3{x}^{2},1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-9{c}_{1}^{2}+8 \\\\ 9{c}_{1}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-(-8)}{0-(-8)} & =\\frac{x-(-2)}{1-(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{y+8}{8} & =\\frac{x+2}{3} \\\\ y+8 & =\\frac{8(x+2)}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8(x-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{8({c}_{1}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=\\frac{8(-1-1)}{3}=-\\frac{16}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $(-1,-\\frac{16}{3}).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=\\frac{8(1-1)}{3}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,0).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $(-1,-\\frac{16}{3})$ and $(1,0).$",
