    "q-106586": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106586",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,0⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,-1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,-1,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 1-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,2y,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨0,2{c}_{2},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 2 & =⟨0,2{c}_{2},-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 2 & =-2{c}_{2}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-1)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x & =-y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t\\,\\,y=-t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t\\,\\,{c}_{2}=-t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $-2{c}_{2}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(-t)+3(1-t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2t+3{t}^{2}-6t+3 & =2 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-4t+1 & =0 \\\\ (3t-1)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(1,-1,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-106586-a",
