    "q-106587": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106587",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-2,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,-2,0⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-2,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,-2,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-2,0)-f(-2,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,-2,0⟩-⟨-2,0,1⟩) \\\\ -4-1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨0,-2y,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨0,-2{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =⟨0,-2{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ -5 & =4{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-(-2)}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x+2}{2} & =-\\frac{y}{2}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=2t-2,\\,y=-2t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2t-2,\\,{c}_{2}=-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4{c}_{2}-2{c}_{3}=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-2t)-2(1-t) & =-5 \\\\ -8t-2+2t & =-5 \\\\ -6t & =-3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})-2=-1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(-1,-1,\\frac{1}{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
